the wrong things in our world of  glamorous fast cars tomato throwing  contests actors instead of the right  things going to bed early long walks  observing the sky at dusk kindness it's  not that nothing at all is glamorous  it's just that we need to direct our  admiration and excitement more wisely we  need to turn it upon the things which  genuinely deserve prestige artists can  help us one of the fundamental things  that art can do for us is turn the  spotlight of glamour in the best and  most helpful directions artists identify  things that we tend to overlook but  which ideally we should care about  deeply serving women bread and milk were  not especially exciting in the late  1650s when Johann Vermeer painted this  picture she wasn't a celebrity he isn't  showing us somebody who was already  highly admired yet Vermeer saw in this  serving woman pouring milk something  that he felt deserved prolonged  contemplation and admiration he thought  something really important was going on  by worldly standards it's a pretty  humble situation but the care with which  she works is moving Vermeer is impressed  by the idea that our true needs might be  really rather simple bread and milk  already rather satisfying the light  coming through the window is beautiful  and a plain white wall can be a major  source of delight Vermeer is  redistributing glamour by raising the  prestige of things he depicts and he's  trying to get us to feel the same way  the milkmaid is a kind of propaganda or  advert for homely pleasures or consider  the painstakingly skilful and commercial  business of lace making Vermeer paint  the self-employed businesswoman with the  usual diversion and care that would be  given to a military hero or a great  political leader Vermeer was himself  unremarkable in many ways he was born in  1632 in the small but beautiful city of  Delft where his father was a modestly  successful art dealer come in peeper he  stayed there most of his life he never  travelled away from Delft after his  marriage at age 20 he hardly even left  his Pleasant home he and his wife  Katharina had 11 surviving children and  he did much of his painting from a room  on the upper floor Vermeer was a slow  painter partially because he was not  only a painter he continued the family  businesses of art dealing and in keeping  and he also became head of the local  Guild of painters in contemporary terms  his career was not a huge success  he wasn't especially famous and he  didn't make a lot of money he was in  fact an exemplary member of what was in  those days a nearly important kind of  person the middle-class individual  Vermeer was in his teens when Holland or  technically the seven provinces became  an independent state  the first burgeois republic in contrast  to the semi-feudal aristocratic nations  that surrounded it Holland gave honour  and political power to people who are  not at the pinnacle of society to  merchants administrators prosperous  artisans and entrepreneurs it was the  first country in the world to be  recognizably modern in this era and a  great insight of Christianity one which  is easily detachable from the  surrounding theology became increasingly  relevant that everybody's inner life is  important even if on the outside they  don't seem to be particularly  distinguished  Vermeer paints the girl with a Pearl  Earring with the same kind of  consideration she isn't anybody famous  or important she isn't rich the earrings  she wears is nice but it's a minor  trinket in the eyes of the fashionable  world it's the one pricey thing she  earns  yet she's not in need of justice she's  not downtrodden or badly treated she is  for want of a better term ordinary yet  she is like everyone not in the least  bit ordinary she is uniquely  profoundly and mysteriously herself the  picture which best thumbs up for me is  philosophy the little street has become  one of the most famous works of art in  the world  it is brightly placed in Amsterdam's  great Rijksmuseum it is insured for  perhaps half a billion euros and it's  the subject of a mountain of Leonard  articles yet the painting is pointedly  out of sync with its status because  above all else it wants to show us that  the ordinary can be very special the  picture says that looking after a simple  but beautiful home cleaning the yard  watching the children diamond cloth and  doing these things faithfully and  without despair is life's real duty it  is an anti-heroic picture a weapon  against the false images of glamour it  refuses to accept that true glamour  depends on amazing feats of courage or  the attainment of status it argues that  doing the modest things the things that  are expected of all of us is enough  Vermeer did not live long  he died in 1675 when he was still only  in his early 40s but he had communicated  a crucial and hugely sane idea much of  what matters to us is not the exciting  urgent or dramatic or special things  most of life has taken up with dealing  with things at a routine modest humble  and to be honest a touch dull our  culture should focus on getting us to  appreciate the average the ordinary and  the everyday when Vermeer painted his  hometown he didn't choose a special day  the sky was neither very overcast nor  especially sunny nothing is happening  there are no celebrities around yet it  is as he has taught us to recognize all  very special indeed  you
 

